//? Title: Grover search: two reflections, a stopping choice
//? Subtitle: Actual floating amplitudes, deterministic argmax and classical simulation work
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
An oracle recognizes one marked item among N possibilities; the aim is high
success probability with few oracle uses. Grover amplitude amplification rotates
a uniform state toward the marked state, but extra iterations can undo that
progress. This report follows actual oracle and diffusion amplitudes on four
states, retains the original 256-state example, and separates an approximate
legacy stopping rule from a first-peak estimate. The implementation is a
classical floating state-vector simulator given the target index. Its historical
measured field is a deterministic argmax, not a sampled measurement, and its CPU
and storage costs differ from the quantum query model.
)

\toc

> Four states, one complete iteration

With two qubits and marked index three, preparation gives four amplitudes
approximately one half. The oracle negates only the marked amplitude. Their mean
becomes approximately one quarter, and diffusion replaces each amplitude a by
twice that mean minus a. The three unmarked amplitudes approach zero while the
marked amplitude approaches one.

The table prints native phase snapshots. For the diagram, the horizontal
coordinate is the amplitude along the normalized uniform unmarked direction, and
the vertical coordinate is the marked amplitude. All three unmarked amplitudes
agree, so that horizontal coordinate is √3 times one of them. The diagram uses
equal scales and actual values; its exact-arithmetic geometry is derived next.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/grover_reflections.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    const auto t = dm::algorithms::grover_trace(2, 3, 1);
    const auto &step = t.steps.at(0);
    std::print("| basis index | initial real amplitude | after oracle | after diffusion |\n| ---: | ---: | " "---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < 4; ++i)
        std::print("| {} | {:.17g} | {:.17g} | {:.17g} |\n", i,
                   t.initial[i].real(), step.after_oracle[i].real(),
                   step.after_diffusion[i].real());
    std::print("\nActual oracle mean {:.17g}; marked squared amplitude {:.17g}; final mass {:.17g}; " "deterministic argmax {}.\n",
               step.oracle_mean.real(), step.marked_probability,
               dm::algorithms::grover_state_mass(t.run.state),
               t.run.result.measured);
    const double x0 =
        std::sqrt(3.) * t.initial[0].real(), y0 =
        t.initial[3].real(), xo =
        std::sqrt(3.) * step.after_oracle[0].real(), yo =
        step.after_oracle[3].real(), xd =
        std::sqrt(3.) * step.after_diffusion[0].real(), yd =
        step.after_diffusion[3].real();
    dm::graphics::Diagram d(230, 1000);
    dm::graphics::DiagramStroke axis;
    axis.color = {148, 163, 184};
    axis.width = 1.2;
    d.path({{-.2, 0}, {1.25, 0}}, axis).path({{0, -.75}, {0, 1.3}}, axis);
    auto reflection = axis;
    reflection.dashed = true;
    d.path({{0, 0}, {1.3 * x0, 1.3 * y0}}, reflection);
    dm::graphics::DiagramStroke arrow;
    arrow.arrow = true;
    arrow.width = 3;
    arrow.color = {37, 99, 235};
    d.path({{0, 0}, {x0, y0}}, arrow);
    arrow.color = {217, 119, 6};
    d.path({{0, 0}, {xo, yo}}, arrow);
    arrow.color = {5, 150, 105};
    d.path({{0, 0}, {xd, yd}}, arrow);
    d.text({1.45, .55}, "Initial state", 23).text({1.45, -.55}, "After oracle",
                                                  23).text({-.4, 1.1},
                                                           "After diffusion", 23).text({1.1, -.14}, "Unmarked direction", 21).text({.6, 1.7}, "Actual states: oracle then diffusion", 25).save_svg("build/grover_reflections.svg");
    if (t.run.result.measured != 3 || step.marked_probability < .999999999)
        throw std::runtime_error("four-state amplification");
}
```
