\caption(The actual sequential mean feeds the captured diffusion; no second execution reconstructs the phases. Mass and probability retain roundoff without renormalization or clamping.)
\alttext(Blue, orange and green arrows show initial, post-oracle and post-diffusion states in the unmarked/marked plane. The oracle reflects the marked coordinate, then diffusion points the state toward the marked axis.)

> Derive the rotation

For one marked basis state \(|w\rangle\), let \(|u\rangle\) be the normalized
uniform superposition of the N−1 unmarked states. For N>1,

$$|s\rangle=\cos\theta\,|u\rangle+\sin\theta\,|w\rangle,
\qquad\sin\theta=N^{-1/2}.$$
\alttext(The initial uniform state has marked coordinate one over square root N and unmarked coordinate square root of one minus one over N.)

In this ordered orthonormal basis, the oracle O flips the marked coordinate, and
diffusion D reflects about the initial state:

$$O=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&-1\end{pmatrix},\qquad
D=2|s\rangle\langle s|-I=\begin{pmatrix}\cos2\theta&\sin2\theta\\\sin2\theta&-\cos2\theta\end{pmatrix}.$$
\alttext(The oracle reflects across the unmarked axis. Diffusion is the reflection matrix about the initial uniform direction.)

Their product is a rotation by twice theta:

$$DO=\begin{pmatrix}\cos2\theta&-\sin2\theta\\\sin2\theta&\cos2\theta\end{pmatrix},\qquad
P_k=\sin^2((2k+1)\theta).$$
\alttext(Two reflections compose to a rotation. The ideal marked probability after k iterations is the square of the sine of two k plus one times theta.)

For N=4, theta=π/6. The initial angle is π/6 and one iteration reaches π/2,
giving ideal probability one. Actual Hadamard and reflection arithmetic
approximates these values in double precision. The symmetry of the unmarked
coordinates is visible in the table, while the printed norm reveals that exact
unitarity is a mathematical model rather than a bit-level claim about the
simulator.

> Choose a first peak, rather than calling an approximation optimal

The first continuous maximum occurs at \(k_*=\pi/(4\theta)-1/2\). A nearby
nonnegative integer gives the first-lobe stopping estimate. The new helper uses
\(\lfloor\pi/(4\theta)\rfloor\), with explicit constant-probability handling for
N=1 and N=2. It is a scalar theory estimate, not an exhaustive search over
arbitrarily late iterations or a guarantee about rounded probabilities.

The legacy `grover` API retains \(\operatorname{round}(\pi\sqrt N/4)\). It is
not generally the best integer choice: for N=4 it takes two steps and returns a
target squared amplitude near one quarter, whereas one step is near unity.
Retaining this schedule makes old results reproducible; `grover_run` accepts an
explicit count.

> Retain the original target 42 among 256 states

Here the legacy count is thirteen and the first-peak estimate is twelve. The
following complete native trace executes forty iterations once. Its window
maximum comes from those actual probabilities; the ideal curve is a separately
named scalar reference. A window maximum is not a global maximum over all future
iterations. Further iterations rotate past the first peak and eventually revisit
high probabilities.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/grover_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    const auto old = dm::algorithms::grover(8, 42);
    const int first = dm::algorithms::grover_first_peak_iterations(8);
    const auto t = dm::algorithms::grover_trace(8, 42, 40);
    std::vector<double> x{0}, p{std::norm(t.initial[42])}, ideal{
        dm::algorithms::grover_ideal_probability(8, 0)
    };
    for (const auto &s : t.steps) {
        x.push_back(s.iteration);
        p.push_back(s.marked_probability);
        ideal.push_back(dm::algorithms::grover_ideal_probability(8, s.iteration));
    }
    const auto peak = std::max_element(p.begin(), p.end()) - p.begin();
    std::print("N=256, target=42. Legacy iterations {}; argmax {}; target squared amplitude {:.12f}.\n",
               old.iterations, old.measured, old.success_prob);
    std::print("First-peak estimate {}; actual probability there {:.12f}; displayed window maximum {:.12f} " "at {}; probability at 40 {:.12f}.\n",
               first, p[first], p[peak], peak, p.back());
    std::print("Sequential classical scan under a uniformly located target, querying even its last " "position: expected checks {}.\n\n",
               (256. + 1) / 2);
    std::print("| iterations | actual target probability | scalar ideal reference | actual state mass |\n| " "---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (int k : {0, 1, 8, 12, 13, 20, 25, 37, 40})
        std::print("| {} | {:.12f} | {:.12f} | {:.12f} |\n", k, p[k], ideal[k],
                   dm::algorithms::grover_state_mass(k
                                                     ? t.steps[k - 1].after_diffusion
                                                     : t.initial));
    auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    plot.line(x, ideal, "ideal rotation", {5, 150, 105}, 3).line(
        x, p, "native state vector", {37, 99, 235}, 1.5).points(
        {double(peak)}, {p[peak]}, "window maximum", {220, 38, 38}, 5).title(
        "Original Grover example: amplification and overshoot").x_label(
        "complete oracle/diffusion iterations").y_label("marked squared amplitude").y_limits(0, 1.3).save_svg("build/grover_original.svg");
    if (old.iterations != 13 || first != 12 || peak != 12 || p[12] <= p[13])
        throw std::runtime_error("original stopping comparison");
}
