\caption(Native probabilities come from actual full-phase records. The green reference is explicitly analytic; matching curves verify the model in this finite example without replacing the state-vector calculation.)
\alttext(The marked probability rises toward one near twelve iterations, falls through an overshoot, then rises again. A red point marks the largest actual probability in the displayed zero-to-forty window.)

The field `measured` is the first argmax of the final squared amplitudes. No
random number is drawn and no state collapse occurs. For N=2, all integer
iteration counts have ideal marked probability one half, and the deterministic
argmax can be zero even when the marked index is one. Returning the target as
the most probable index in a larger example does not mean a sampled measurement
must succeed.

The classical comparison above specifies its stopping protocol and a uniform
target location; its expectation is (N+1)/2, or 128.5 checks at N=256. The
quantum query model counts uses of a supplied phase oracle and allows failure
probability. State preparation, oracle synthesis, verification and hardware
costs require additional assumptions. This classical simulator is given the
target index and updates every amplitude, so that query comparison is not a CPU
benchmark or a claim of implemented quantum speedup.

> Count the classical work separately

Let N=2ⁿ and K be complete iterations. Preparation applies n Hadamards to N/2
amplitude pairs each. Every subsequent oracle negates one known target entry,
then the mean and diffusion loops each visit all N amplitudes. A final argmax
scans N candidates:

$$H_{pairs}=nN/2,\quad Q=K,\quad M=KN,\quad U=KN,\quad A=N.$$
\alttext(Preparation touches n times N over two amplitude pairs. Oracle calls count K, while mean and diffusion each visit K times N amplitudes, and final argmax visits N.)

These are selected logical visits, not scalar FLOPs or physical gate costs.
Uniform initialization, probability extraction, snapshot copies and allocation
have their own costs. Ordinary simulation uses O(nN+KN) work and O(N)
state/output storage. With K proportional to √N, the iteration work is
O(N^(3/2)); the quantum oracle-use model instead counts O(√N) calls. Input bit
count n therefore produces exponentially growing classical simulation work and
storage.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/grover_work.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    std::print("| qubits n | amplitudes N | legacy count | chosen first-peak count | H pairs | mean visits " "| diffusion visits | final argmax visits | state payload bytes |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | " "---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    std::vector<double> x, queries, visits;
    for (int n = 2; n <= 12; ++n) {
        const auto N = std::size_t{1} << n;
        const int k = dm::algorithms::grover_first_peak_iterations(n);
        const auto r = dm::algorithms::grover_run(n, N - 1, k);
        const auto &w = r.work;
        if (w.hadamard_pairs != std::size_t(n) * (N / 2) ||
            w.oracle_calls != std::size_t(k) || w.mean_terms != N * k ||
            w.diffusion_updates != N * k || w.argmax_terms != N)
            throw std::runtime_error("operator visits");
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", n, N,
                   dm::algorithms::grover(n, N - 1).iterations, k,
                   w.hadamard_pairs, w.mean_terms, w.diffusion_updates,
                   w.argmax_terms, N * sizeof(dm::algorithms::Complex));
        x.push_back(n);
        queries.push_back(std::log2(double(w.oracle_calls)));
        visits.push_back(std::log2(double(w.mean_terms + w.diffusion_updates)));
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.line(x, queries, "oracle calls", {220, 38, 38}, 2).line(
        x, visits, "mean + diffusion visits", {37, 99, 235}, 2).title(
        "Simulator visits and abstract oracle-use counts").x_label("qubits n").y_label("log2(selected count)").y_limits(0, 24).save_svg("build/grover_work.svg");
}
```
