One-short complete capture rejects: true; exact cap retains 13 complex values; invalid target rejected before state cap: true; negative trace length rejected: true; snapshots copy independently: true.
\image(figures/grover_controls.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The four cases preserve the legacy schedule and show explicitly selected alternatives. The near-unit bars retain native floating values rather than being replaced by analytic ones or clipped to one.)
\alttext(Paired bars compare legacy and explicit first-peak target probabilities. The two-qubit case changes from about one quarter to near one, while the one-qubit case stays near one half.)

The new run/trace APIs default to a complete state cap of 2²⁰ complex
amplitudes; zero disables that policy cap. Legacy entry points have no optional
state cap, but shifts, target indices and container sizes are checked before
allocation. Negative qubit or iteration counts are rejected. The full trace
preflights the known requirement

$$C=N+K(2N+1).$$
\alttext(Capture retains the initial N amplitudes, then two N-amplitude phase states and one complex mean per iteration.)

The default capture cap is one million complex-value units, zero disabling it.
It excludes step metadata, probability fields, ordinary final output, capacities
and allocation overhead; it is not a byte or peak-RAM limit. Known requirements
are checked before gates or state allocation. Failure returns no partial trace.
Initial, oracle, diffused and ordinary final states own independent storage. No
input references or random-sampling state survive.

The single-step compatibility helper also accepts a nonempty finite complex
vector outside the normal prepared state, validates it before mutation, and can
leave a partial change on arithmetic overflow. Public simulations use owned
local states, preserving their ordinary state-vector arithmetic without
probability normalization. Independent calls share no mutable state. The rest of
the quantum simulator retains its own contracts.

> What the examples establish

Two native reflections explain amplification, and a finite actual trace shows
overshoot. Correct stopping needs an integer choice and a stated success
criterion. The old default is retained as an approximation, while an explicit
count makes the comparison reviewable. Query complexity concerns an oracle
model; this emulator's work and storage concern full classical vectors.
Synthetic basis states are the problem itself here, so an unrelated dataset
would add no evidence.

The primary source is L. K. Grover, [A fast quantum mechanical algorithm for
database search](https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9605043), presented at STOC1996;
the revised November1996 version explains inversion about the average. This
report derives the two-reflection model for one marked state and separately
measures the library's floating implementation. It makes no device-performance,
cryptographic-resource or arbitrary-many-marked-state claim.

Verification compares legacy summaries and scalar probability traces bit for bit
with a frozen pre-conversion implementation, reconstructs actual captured
phases, checks selected work and complete caps, and compares against independent
matrix/high-precision calculations. Invalid shifts/targets, zero counts, argmax
ties, ownership and numerical boundaries accompany sanitizer checks. Complete
programs, inspected local figures, API examples and both exports are verified
before retiring the original report.
